  "prediction": "**Summary of the Department of Defense (DOD) Workforce Report**\n\nThe Department of Defense (DOD) has undergone significant changes in its workforce composition and size since 2001. The DOD's military, civilian, and contractor workforces peaked around 2011 and have since decreased in size. The report highlights the challenges faced by the DOD in managing its workforce, including varying levels of U.S. involvement in conflicts, military to civilian and contractor conversions, and the growth of certain workforces such as acquisition and cyber.\n\nThe report assesses the DOD's efforts to compare the costs of civilian and contractor personnel, as mandated by Congress. The DOD's Cost-Comparison Report partially addressed the reporting element to account for the full cost of federal civilian and contractor personnel. The report found that the costs of federal civilians and service contractors were not significantly different, but the results were not generalizable across the department.\n\nThe report also highlights the challenges faced by the DOD in determining the functions performed by contractor personnel, which is similar to the challenges encountered in prior work on DOD's efforts to compile and review an inventory of contracted services.\n\nThe report makes several recommendations to the DOD, including:\n\n1. Assess the advantages and disadvantages of allowing the continued use of different cost-estimation tools across the department or directing department-wide application of one tool.\n2. Revise its guidance in accordance with the findings of its assessment.\n3. Improve its methodology for estimating and comparing the full cost of work performed by military and civilian personnel and contractor support.\n4. Develop a comprehensive plan to reduce the number of contractor personnel and increase the use of civilian personnel.\n5. Improve its inventory of contracted services to better manage these services.\n\nThe report concludes that the DOD has made progress in addressing some of the challenges faced by its workforce, but more work is needed to ensure that the department is using its resources efficiently and effectively.\n\n**Key Findings:**\n\n* The DOD's workforce has decreased in size since 2011.\n* The DOD's Cost-Comparison Report partially addressed the reporting element to account for the full cost of federal civilian and contractor personnel.\n* The report highlights the challenges faced by the DOD in determining the functions performed by contractor personnel.\n* The report makes several recommendations to the DOD to improve its workforce management.\n\n**Recommendations:**\n\n* Assess the advantages and disadvantages of allowing the continued use of different cost-estimation tools across the department or directing department-wide application of one tool.\n* Revise its guidance in",
